                                    <h2><span class="shaft"><img id="1281-0" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_o.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Omnidirectional" title="Omnidirectional" ><img id="1281-1" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_c.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Cardioid" title="Cardioid" ><img id="1281-2" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_8.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Bidirectional" title="Bidirectional" ></span>Audio-Technica AT4050/LE</h2>
                    
                    <h2 class="sub">                    Multi-Pattern Condenser Microphone</h2>
                    <p>The AT4050/LE is one of two limited-edition versions of the company&#8217;s popular AT4050 multipattern condenser. It differs from the standard version only cosmetically: a metallic silver finish with blue accents, an etched serial number, and a wooden case.</p><p>See also the AT4050 URUSHI.</p><p>Like the original AT4050, the LE is based on a transformerless output circuit and a medium-diaphragm capsule. The dual 21.4mm diaphragms are made of 2-micron plastic film, metallized with gold.</p><p>A switch on the microphone body provides access to three polar patterns: cardioid, omni, bidirectional. Additional switches enable a pad (-10dB) and a high-pass filter (-12dB/octave @ 80Hz).</p><p><a rel="shadowbox" href="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images//mic_extras/audio-technica/AT4050-set.jpg"><img src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/mic_extras/audio-technica/AT4050-set-sm.jpg" class="thumb shaft" alt="AT4050URUSHI" width="200" /></a>The LE ships with a wooden case, a dust cover, and an elastic shockmount (p/n AT8449).<p>The <a href="//recordinghacks.com/microphones/Audio-Technica/AT4050LE">Audio-Technica AT4050/LE</a> is also known as: AT4050LE.</p><p>The mic was released in 2012.</p>
